Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia (ALL)
Haematology & Oncology
Abrupt severe fatigue/fever, Bone pain, Petechiae/easy bruising, >20% lymphoblasts on bone marrow

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-001
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of unremitting bone pain, new petechial rash, and persistent fever in a paediatric patient requiring urgent evaluation to exclude rapidly progressive systemic infection or acute haematological malignancy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver (mother)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute evolution of unexplained bruising, fever, and severe bone pain. Urgent in-person assessment was mandated to evaluate for severe thrombocytopenia and impending clinical deterioration, as the assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They just won't walk today and keep crying that their legs hurt. I also noticed these tiny red spots all over their stomach this evening."
History of Present Illness: The caregiver reports an abrupt 48-hour history of severe fatigue and lethargy in the 4-year-old child. Initially starting as generalized tiredness, the symptoms progressed linearly to a refusal to weight-bear yesterday due to bilateral leg pain. This evening, the caregiver noted a new, non-blanching pinpoint rash on the abdomen and recurrent low-grade fevers. No obvious trauma.
Functional Status: Bedbound/carried; refusing to mobilize due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High parental anxiety; risk of rapid physiological decline overnight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 125 | Temp: 37.9 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 16 Kg
General: Pale, fatigued, and irritable child resting in caregiver's arms. Mild distress upon lower limb movement.
Physical Exam:
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm, no murmurs.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no work of breathing.
Skin: Widespread petechiae on the abdomen and lower limbs. Easily bruising noted on the shins.
Musculoskeletal: Diffuse tenderness upon palpation of the bilateral tibiae and femora.
Lymphatic: Generalized, non-tender cervical and axillary lymphadenopathy.
Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, with palpable firm hepatosplenomegaly extending 2 cm below the costal margins.
Environmental Observations: Safe sleep environment; digital thermometer accessible.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 240mg given 3 hours ago with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia (ALL) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Meningococcal Septicaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: insidious 48-hour timeline, normal perfusion, and absence of purpura fulminans or profound shock.

Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura (ITP) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: presence of significant bone pain, hepatosplenomegaly, and lymphadenopathy.

Osteomyelitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: bilateral and diffuse nature of the bone pain, lack of localized erythema, and generalized systemic signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The clinical presentation of abrupt severe fatigue, fever, generalized bone pain, and petechiae in a paediatric patient constitutes a high-risk scenario. Objective findings of hepatosplenomegaly, generalized lymphadenopathy, and a non-blanching rash raise immediate suspicion for bone marrow infiltration and severe thrombocytopenia. Delaying this assessment until regular clinic hours would pose an unacceptable risk of fatal haemorrhage or overwhelming sepsis due to profound neutropenia. This presentation explicitly satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours home visit to stratify risk and initiate an immediate escalation of care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate referral to the Paediatric Emergency Department for full blood count, peripheral smear, coagulation profile, and potential bone marrow aspiration (evaluating for >20% lymphoblasts).
Positive Behavioral Change:

Maintain strict hand hygiene and minimize the child's contact with others. By reducing exposure to pathogens, you prevent secondary infections while their immune system is compromised.

Avoid administering any NSAIDs (like ibuprofen). By strictly using paracetamol for pain, you prevent further impairment of platelet function and reduce the risk of spontaneous bleeding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required now. Call 000 if the child experiences sudden altered conscious state, massive bleeding from the gums or nose, or respiratory distress during transit.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is presumptive pending definitive blood tests and specialist investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The current symptoms represent a systemic process that will acutely worsen without specialist haematology intervention.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Proceed directly to the nearest Paediatric Emergency Department. Do not wait for the morning. Inform the triage nurse of the bone pain, petechial rash, and fever.

